I have noticed that monsters with weapons can do an inordinate amount
of damage.  For instance, I was just now playing a character who
happened to have 10 HP's left when he encountered a hill orc toting a
scimitar.  The orc took one swipe at my character, and poof, instant
bones level.  I wouldn't have given him the chance, but he just killed
my cat, and I was really pissed.

Now, a quick look at the source shows that a scimitar, in my hand, can
do 1-8 points of damage.  In D&D, monsters using weapons do damage
according to the weapon type.  I notice that monsters with weapons
consistently do more damage than the weapons do when my characters use
them.  For instance, a dwarf holding a dwarvish mattock (huge shiny
sword) can kill a character with 50 HP's in just a couple of swipes.

Why is this?  I haven't taken a long look at the source to see how
monster damage works, but why should monsters be able to do more
damage than characters with the same weapons?

As a note, I can deal with this.  If I kill some monster carrying a
weapon, I'll always pick up the weapon to keep other monsters from
using it.  If I'm carrying too much stuff to haul all these weapons
around, I'll go up a level and dump them in a pile there before going
back to the current level, just to keep them from being used against
me.  You can also dump them in a box or a chest.

Armed monsters are so much more dangerous than the unarmed ones, I
think it might be worthwhile to identify them as such when you use the
"/" command to get information about an object on the screen.

